  "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x-2}+1 & <3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x-2} & <2 \\\\ {e}^{x-2} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{x-2}) & <\\operatorname{ln}\u20611 \\\\ x-2 & <0 \\\\ x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
